The advisory is spot on - the content-type is set via `FetchBodyUtility.getBodyStream()` which returns content-type from the streamed body content. But when the body is a ReadableStream (like our createHangingStream()), `getBodyStream` might not have a content-type. The explicit headers set in `init.headers` are handled separately - they get set via `this.headers.set('Content-Type', contentType)` but only if `contentType` (from getBodyStream) is truthy and the header isn't already set.

Wait, let me re-read the code:

```javascript
const { stream, buffer, contentType, contentLength } = FetchBodyUtility.getBodyStream(
    this[PropertySymbol.window],
    body
);

if (contentType) {
    if (!this.headers.has('Content-Type')) {
        this.headers.set('Content-Type', contentType);
    }
    this[PropertySymbol.contentType] = contentType;
}
```

So `contentType` comes from `getBodyStream`. For a ReadableStream body, `getBodyStream` likely returns `contentType: null` because a ReadableStream has no inherent content type. So `this[PropertySymbol.contentType]` stays null.

Then in `formData()`:
```javascript
const contentType = this[PropertySymbol.contentType]; // null!
if (this.body && contentType && /multipart/i.test(contentType)) {
    // never entered because contentType is null
}
```

Even though the Content-Type header IS set on the request (from `init.headers['Content-Type']`), the `this[PropertySymbol.contentType]` symbol is separate and is only set from `getBodyStream`, not from the request headers.

Response.formData() uses `this.headers.get('Content-Type')` instead:
```javascript
const contentType = this.headers.get('Content-Type');
```

So Response.formData() works correctly while Request.formData() uses the wrong source.

The fix is to change Request.formData() to use `this.headers.get('Content-Type')` for the multipart detection, consistent with Response.formData().

